JamesDSP (rootless) -- replaces AudioFX in this ROM.

The APK is NOT committed here. Run ../../fetch-jamesdsp.sh to fetch and verify
it; that script also writes the Android.bp next to the APK. Until it is run this directory holds only this README, so the tree
still builds and AudioFX stays in place.

Pinned artifact
    me.timschneeberger.rootlessjamesdsp  1.6.14 (versionCode 51)
    https://f-droid.org/repo/me.timschneeberger.rootlessjamesdsp_51.apk
    sha256 4cdd6cbc57abb00b1ce28668dc29c021640bae8c4521ec592efc58c4239d83a1
    38,647,450 bytes, minSdk 29, targetSdk 34, arm64-v8a + armeabi-v7a

Why F-Droid and not GitHub: the upstream GitHub releases carry no APK assets at
all -- the project publishes through F-Droid, which builds from source and
serves a stable, checksummable URL per versionCode.

WHAT THIS CHANGES, AND THE HONEST TRADE-OFF
    AudioFX drives the VENDOR audio effects (equalizer, bass boost, virtualizer)
    through the platform AudioEffect API. JamesDSP rootless does not: it captures
    playback with the AudioPlaybackCapture API, processes it, and re-renders.

    Consequences, none of which are bugs:
      - apps that set allowAudioPlaybackCapture="false" (most DRM video, some
        streaming apps) CANNOT be processed, by Android design
      - a small amount of added latency
      - a persistent foreground-service notification

    It is a far more capable DSP than AudioFX in exchange for those. If the
    vendor effect chain matters more than the DSP, keep AudioFX and skip this.

NOT PRIVILEGED, AND WHAT THAT COSTS
    Without android.permission.DUMP the app cannot see audio sessions on its
    own, so the user must re-accept a MediaProjection capture dialog after EVERY
    reboot. The app works; that is the cost.

    It USED to ship privileged, with a generated privapp-permissions allowlist
    granting exactly DUMP. 🔴 That combination boot-looped the device, measured
    on hardware in build 51:

        FATAL EXCEPTION IN SYSTEM PROCESS: main
        java.lang.IllegalStateException: Signature|privileged permissions not in
          privapp-permissions allowlist: {me.timschneeberger.rootlessjamesdsp
          (/product/priv-app/JamesDSP): android.permission.DUMP}

    system_server reached ~238 services -- effectively a complete boot -- then
    threw and restarted, forever. It had been latent since the app was added on
    2026-08-05, because every build until then was killed by Watchdog at the
    audio HAL before PackageManagerService.systemReady() was reached.

    🔴 Why the allowlist was ignored is UNEXPLAINED. It was present at
    /product/etc/permissions/, 0644, u:object_r:system_file:s0, well-formed,
    naming exactly that package and permission; SystemConfig logged reading it
    on every boot; there was no parse warning; and bind-mounting a copy into
    /system/etc/permissions as well did not help. Do not simply restore
    privileged:true -- explain that first.
